# A Guided Laboratory for Hyperbolic Coxeter Growth

Hyperbolic reflection geometry turns discrete angle labels into curved spaces with cusps. Begin with the local numerical grammar: choose an integer order and watch the allowed Gram entry move through the interval $(-1,0]$.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the Gram-entry argument</summary>
For $m\ge2$, one has $0<\pi/m\le\pi/2$. Hence $0\le\cos(\pi/m)<1$, and negation gives $-1<-\cos(\pi/m)\le0$. This is necessary local data, not a complete realization criterion.
</details>

## Count fingerprints instead of comparing every pair

A commensurability invariant is constant on each class. It therefore factors through the quotient, so the number of invariant values cannot exceed the number of classes. Explore the exact decorated binary model below.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal why the model is nondegenerate</summary>
Each binary word has two decorations. Equivalence forgets the decoration, so two unequal objects occupy every class. The invariant remembers the word and takes exactly $2^n$ values.
</details>

## Turn linear cost into exponential abundance

If every one of $n$ binary choices costs at most $A$ volume, with overhead $B$, then $V\le An+B$ while the class count is at least $2^n$. The corresponding exponent is $(\log2)/A$.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the transfer theorem</summary>
If polytopes $P_w$, indexed by $w\in\{0,1\}^n$, satisfy $\operatorname{vol}(P_w)\le An+B$ and have pairwise distinct values of a commensurability invariant, then they represent at least $2^n$ classes. At $V=An+B$, this equals $\exp((\log2)(V-B)/A)$.
</details>

## Continue exploring

Use {{algorithm:1}} to screen candidate angle data, then consult the [Wikipedia introduction to Coxeter groups](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coxeter_group), the [hyperbolic-space overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyperbolic_space), and the [commensurability overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commensurability_(group_theory)) for broader background. The numerical widgets illustrate necessary conditions and counting consequences; geometric realization still requires signature, incidence, and vertex-link checks.

